PART 1 — WHAT IS AN OPERATING SYSTEM?
1. What is OS?

An Operating System is system software that acts as an intermediary between:

USER
 ↓
APPLICATIONS
 ↓
OPERATING SYSTEM
 ↓
HARDWARE

Examples:

Windows
Linux
macOS
Android
Unix
Simple example

You open Chrome.

You don't directly tell the CPU:

"Give me RAM."

Instead:

Chrome
 ↓
Operating System
 ↓
RAM / CPU / Disk / Network

The OS manages those resources.

Main responsibilities

Remember:

OS = Resource Manager + Control Program

It manages:

CPU
Memory
Files
I/O devices
Processes
Security/protection
2. KERNEL

The kernel is the core part of an operating system.

Operating System
│
├── Kernel
├── Shell / Interface
├── File management
├── Device management
└── Other services

The kernel directly manages hardware resources.

Important

Kernel ≠ entire OS

Kernel is the core of the OS.

3. SHELL

Shell provides an interface through which users interact with the OS.

Example:

User
 ↓
Shell
 ↓
Kernel
 ↓
Hardware

Examples:

Bash
PowerShell
Command Prompt

A previous SSC Scientific Assistant question asked which program interacts with the inner part called the kernel; the answer was Shell.

Memory trick

Shell = talks to Kernel

4. SYSTEM CALLS ⭐⭐⭐

This is extremely important.

A system call is a mechanism through which a user program requests a service from the operating system/kernel.

Example:

Program
   ↓
System Call
   ↓
Kernel
   ↓
Hardware/resource

Suppose a program wants to read a file.

It doesn't directly access the disk.

It requests:

read()

The OS handles it.

Types of system calls

Remember these categories:

1. Process control

Examples:

create process
terminate process
execute program
wait
2. File management

Examples:

create file
open
read
write
close
3. Device management

Examples:

request device
release device
read device
write device
4. Information maintenance

Examples:

get time
get system information
5. Communication

Used for communication between processes.

6. Protection

Controls access to resources.

Exam trap

BIOS is NOT a type of system call.

A previous OS question specifically uses this distinction.

5. USER MODE vs KERNEL MODE ⭐⭐⭐

Modern CPUs generally provide different privilege levels.

The important distinction:

USER MODE
   ↓
limited privileges

KERNEL MODE
   ↓
high privileges

Why?

Security.

Imagine every application could directly modify:

RAM
CPU control registers
disk
other programs' memory

That would be dangerous.

So:

Application
   ↓
User Mode
   ↓
System Call
   ↓
Kernel Mode
   ↓
Resource
Exam point

A system call causes controlled transition from user mode to kernel mode.

PART 2 — PROGRAM vs PROCESS ⭐⭐⭐

This is one of the most important OS concepts.

Program

A program is a passive set of instructions stored somewhere.

Example:

chrome.exe

just sitting on disk.

It is not executing.

Process

A process is a program in execution.

Remember:

Program = passive

Process = active

Example:

Chrome installed
       ↓
     Program

Chrome running
       ↓
     Process
6. PROCESS STATES ⭐⭐⭐

A process normally moves through states.

Basic model:

             admit
 NEW ----------------> READY
                         |
                         | scheduler
                         ↓
                       RUNNING
                      /       \
             I/O wait         exit
                ↓               ↓
             WAITING         TERMINATED
                |
             I/O complete
                |
                ↓
              READY
States
NEW

Process is being created.

READY

Process is ready to execute but waiting for CPU.

RUNNING

Currently executing on CPU.

WAITING / BLOCKED

Waiting for some event, commonly I/O.

TERMINATED

Finished execution.

7. READY vs WAITING ⭐⭐⭐

Very common MCQ.

READY

Process wants CPU.

"I am ready. Give me CPU."
WAITING

Process is waiting for an event/resource.

"I cannot continue until I/O finishes."
Memory trick

READY → waiting for CPU

WAITING → waiting for event/I/O

8. PROCESS CONTROL BLOCK — PCB ⭐⭐⭐

The OS needs information about every process.

That information is stored in:

PCB = Process Control Block

Think of PCB as the process's identity card/report card.

It can contain:

Process ID
Process state
Program counter
CPU registers
Scheduling information
Memory-management information
I/O status information
Accounting information
Why PCB is important?

Suppose:

Process A running

CPU switches to:

Process B

The OS must save A's state.

That information is stored in A's PCB.

Then B's information is loaded.

This is part of:

Context switching

9. CONTEXT SWITCH ⭐⭐⭐

Suppose:

CPU
 ↓
Process A

OS decides to run B.

It does approximately:

Save A's context
       ↓
Load B's context
       ↓
Run B

This is a context switch.

Context contains things such as:
registers
program counter
process state
Important

Context switching is overhead.

It doesn't directly perform useful application work.

PART 3 — THREADS ⭐⭐⭐

A thread is the basic unit of CPU execution within a process.

Think:

PROCESS
│
├── Thread 1
├── Thread 2
└── Thread 3

Multiple threads can belong to the same process.

10. PROCESS vs THREAD
Process

Generally has its own:

address space
resources
execution context
Threads in same process

Share many process resources such as:

code
data
open files

But each thread has its own execution-related state such as:

program counter
registers
stack
Easy analogy

Think of a restaurant.

Restaurant = Process

Workers = Threads

Workers share:

Kitchen
Tables
Ingredients

but each worker has their own:

task
temporary working state
SSC trap

A thread shares resources with other threads belonging to the same process.

11. USER-LEVEL vs KERNEL-LEVEL THREADS
User-level threads

Managed mainly by user-level thread library.

Advantages:

fast creation
fast switching

Potential disadvantage:

if one thread blocks under some models, the whole process may be affected.
Kernel-level threads

Managed by OS kernel.

Advantages:

OS knows about them
better support for parallelism depending on implementation
PART 4 — PROCESS SCHEDULING ⭐⭐⭐

CPU scheduling decides:

Which ready process gets CPU next?

12. FCFS

First Come First Serve

First process arriving gets CPU first.

Example:

P1 → P2 → P3

If they arrive in this order:

P1 runs
P2 runs
P3 runs
Advantage

Simple.

Disadvantage

Can produce large waiting times.

13. SJF ⭐⭐⭐

Shortest Job First

Process with smallest CPU burst is selected first.

Example:

P1 = 8 ms
P2 = 3 ms
P3 = 5 ms

Order:

P2 → P3 → P1
Very important fact

SJF gives the minimum average waiting time under the standard assumptions when burst times are known.

A previous SSC Scientific Assistant question tested exactly this concept.

14. SRTF

Shortest Remaining Time First

This is the preemptive version of SJF.

If a new process arrives with a shorter remaining time, the currently running process can be interrupted.

SJF
↓
Non-preemptive

SRTF
↓
Preemptive
15. ROUND ROBIN ⭐⭐⭐

Used especially in time-sharing systems.

Each process gets a fixed:

Time Quantum

Example:

Quantum = 2 ms

P1 → 2 ms
P2 → 2 ms
P3 → 2 ms
P1 → 2 ms
...
If quantum is too large

Round Robin starts behaving more like FCFS.

If quantum is too small

Too many context switches.

16. PRIORITY SCHEDULING

CPU goes to the process with highest priority according to the scheduling convention.

⚠️ Exam trap:

Some systems define:

smaller number = higher priority

Others may define the reverse.

So always check the question's convention.

Problem

Starvation

A low-priority process may wait for a very long time.

Solution

Aging

Gradually increase the priority of a waiting process.

17. IMPORTANT SCHEDULING TERMS
Arrival Time

When process enters ready queue.

Burst Time

CPU time required.

Completion Time

When execution finishes.

Turnaround Time
TAT = Completion Time - Arrival Time
Waiting Time
WT = Turnaround Time - Burst Time
Response Time
Response Time =
First CPU allocation - Arrival Time

⭐ Memorize these.

PART 5 — IPC ⭐⭐⭐

IPC = Inter-Process Communication

Processes sometimes need to communicate.

Two major mechanisms:

1. Shared Memory

Processes share a region of memory.

Process A
    ↓
Shared Memory
    ↑
Process B

Fast, but synchronization is required.

2. Message Passing

Processes communicate by sending messages.

Process A
   ↓ message
Process B
PART 6 — CONCURRENCY ⭐⭐⭐

Concurrency means multiple tasks/processes make progress during overlapping periods.

Example:

P1 ------
       P2 ------
            P3 ------

On a single CPU, the CPU may switch rapidly between processes.

On multiple CPUs/cores, actual parallel execution may occur.

Important distinction

Concurrency ≠ necessarily parallelism

Concurrency = overlapping progress.

Parallelism = actually executing simultaneously on multiple processing units.

18. RACE CONDITION ⭐⭐⭐

Suppose two threads access shared data:

x = 5

Both try:

x = x + 1

If operations interleave badly, final result may not be what we expect.

This is a:

Race condition

The result depends on timing/order of execution.

19. CRITICAL SECTION ⭐⭐⭐

A critical section is a part of a program where shared resources/data are accessed.

Example:

balance = balance - 100;

If multiple processes access balance, this operation may need protection.

Critical-section requirements

Remember:

Mutual exclusion
Progress
Bounded waiting
20. MUTUAL EXCLUSION

Only one process/thread can enter the critical section at a time.

Critical Section

Thread A → ENTER

Thread B → WAIT
21. SEMAPHORE ⭐⭐⭐

A semaphore is a synchronization mechanism.

Two classic operations:

wait()
signal()

Often represented conceptually as:

wait(S)
signal(S)
Binary semaphore

Values commonly:

0 or 1

Often used similarly to a mutex for mutual exclusion, though semaphores and mutexes are not identical concepts.

Counting semaphore

Can represent multiple available instances of a resource.

Example:

5 printers

Semaphore:

S = 5
22. MUTEX

Mutex = mutual exclusion lock.

Conceptually:

lock()
 ↓
critical section
 ↓
unlock()

Only one thread owns the lock at a time.

Exam distinction
Mutex → ownership-style lock
Semaphore → signaling/counting mechanism
PART 7 — DEADLOCK ⭐⭐⭐

Very important.

Deadlock occurs when processes are permanently waiting for resources held by each other.

Example:

P1 holds Printer
P1 waits for Scanner

P2 holds Scanner
P2 waits for Printer

Neither can continue.

P1 → waiting for P2
↑              ↓
└──────────────┘
23. FOUR NECESSARY CONDITIONS OF DEADLOCK ⭐⭐⭐

This is a must memorize topic.

Deadlock requires all four:

1. Mutual Exclusion

Resource cannot be simultaneously shared.

2. Hold and Wait

Process holds one resource while waiting for another.

3. No Preemption

Resource cannot simply be forcibly taken away.

4. Circular Wait
P1 → P2 → P3 → P1
Memory trick

M H N C

Mutual exclusion
Hold and wait
No preemption
Circular wait

If you break any one of these conditions, deadlock can be prevented.

24. DEADLOCK vs STARVATION
Deadlock

Processes are waiting for each other/resources in a deadlock situation.

Starvation

A process waits indefinitely because other processes keep getting the resource/CPU.

Memory
Deadlock → everyone involved gets stuck

Starvation → one process may keep getting ignored
25. DEADLOCK HANDLING

Four broad approaches:

Prevention

Ensure at least one necessary condition cannot hold.

Avoidance

Make resource-allocation decisions carefully so the system remains in a safe state.

Classic algorithm:

Banker's Algorithm

Detection

Allow deadlock and detect it later.

Recovery

After detection, take corrective action.

PART 8 — MEMORY MANAGEMENT ⭐⭐⭐

Now extremely important.

CPU executes processes.

Processes need memory.

OS manages memory allocation.

26. LOGICAL vs PHYSICAL ADDRESS
Logical address

Address generated by CPU/program.

Physical address

Actual address in main memory.

CPU
 ↓
Logical Address
 ↓
MMU
 ↓
Physical Address
 ↓
RAM

MMU:

Memory Management Unit

27. CONTIGUOUS MEMORY ALLOCATION

A process gets one continuous block of memory.

Example:

RAM

| OS | P1 | P1 | P1 | P2 | P2 | Free |

Problem:

Fragmentation

28. INTERNAL FRAGMENTATION ⭐⭐⭐

Allocated memory contains unused space inside the allocated block.

Example:

Process needs:

18 KB

Allocated block:

20 KB

Unused:

2 KB

That 2 KB is internal fragmentation.

Memory

Internal = inside allocated block

29. EXTERNAL FRAGMENTATION ⭐⭐⭐

Free memory exists, but it is scattered into separate small blocks.

Example:

| P1 | 5KB free | P2 | 4KB free | P3 | 6KB free |

Total free:

15 KB

But a process requiring:

12 KB contiguous

may not fit.

Memory

External = outside blocks / scattered holes

30. PAGING ⭐⭐⭐

Paging solves much of the external-fragmentation problem.

Physical memory is divided into:

Frames

Logical memory is divided into:

Pages

Logical memory       Physical memory

Page 0 ───────────→ Frame 3
Page 1 ───────────→ Frame 7
Page 2 ───────────→ Frame 1
Page 3 ───────────→ Frame 9

Pages need not be physically adjacent.

31. PAGE TABLE

OS maintains mapping:

Page Number → Frame Number

Example:

Page 0 → Frame 5
Page 1 → Frame 2
Page 2 → Frame 8
32. PAGE OFFSET

Logical address is divided conceptually into:

Page Number | Offset

Page number identifies page.

Offset identifies location inside that page.

33. SEGMENTATION

Segmentation divides memory according to logical program units.

Example:

Code
Data
Stack

Unlike fixed-size pages, segments can have different sizes.

Paging
fixed-size pages
Segmentation
logical variable-size segments
34. PAGING vs SEGMENTATION
Paging	Segmentation
Fixed-size pages	Variable-size segments
Programmer doesn't usually see pages as logical units	Reflects logical program units
Uses frames	Uses segments
Reduces external fragmentation	Can suffer external fragmentation
PART 9 — VIRTUAL MEMORY ⭐⭐⭐

Virtual memory allows a process to have an address space larger than the available physical RAM, using secondary storage as part of the mechanism.

Example:

Program
 ↓
Virtual Address Space
 ↓
RAM + Disk backing

Only needed parts may be brought into RAM.

35. DEMAND PAGING

Pages are loaded into memory when needed.

Suppose:

Page 1 → RAM
Page 2 → RAM
Page 3 → not in RAM

Program requests Page 3.

That produces:

Page Fault

36. PAGE FAULT ⭐⭐⭐

A page fault occurs when the required page is not currently in physical memory.

Basic process:

CPU requests page
       ↓
Page not in RAM
       ↓
PAGE FAULT
       ↓
OS loads page from storage
       ↓
Page table updated
       ↓
Instruction continues
37. PAGE REPLACEMENT

What if RAM is full?

OS needs to choose a page to remove.

Important algorithms:

FIFO

Remove oldest page.

Optimal

Remove page whose next use is farthest in the future.

This gives the theoretical minimum page faults, but requires future knowledge, so it is generally not directly implementable in a real system.

LRU

Remove page that has not been used for the longest time.

38. BELADY'S ANOMALY ⭐⭐

Interesting exam concept.

In FIFO, increasing the number of frames can sometimes increase the number of page faults.

This is:

Belady's anomaly

Remember:

FIFO → Belady's anomaly possible
39. THRASHING ⭐⭐⭐

When the system spends too much time handling page faults and too little time doing useful work.

Too many page faults
        ↓
Too much disk I/O
        ↓
Very little useful CPU work
        ↓
THRASHING
Memory trick

Thrashing = system is busy swapping pages instead of doing useful work

