47. INTERRUPT ⭐⭐⭐

An interrupt is a signal/event that causes the CPU to temporarily stop its current execution flow and handle an event.

Example:

CPU running
   ↓
Keyboard event
   ↓
Interrupt
   ↓
Interrupt handler
   ↓
Continue
48. DMA ⭐⭐⭐

DMA:

Direct Memory Access

Allows certain I/O devices to transfer data to/from memory with reduced CPU involvement in the actual data movement.

Conceptually:

Without DMA:

Device → CPU → Memory

With DMA:

Device → DMA Controller → Memory
                    ↑
             CPU sets up transfer
Exam point

DMA improves efficiency for large data transfers because CPU doesn't have to move every byte/word itself.

49. SPOOLING ⭐⭐

Spooling = placing jobs/data in a buffer/queue, often on disk, so a device can process them later.

Classic example:

Printer queue

Program A ─┐
Program B ─┼→ Printer Queue → Printer
Program C ─┘
PART 12 — OS TYPES ⭐⭐

Know these basic distinctions.

Batch OS

Jobs processed in batches.

Multiprogramming OS

Multiple programs kept in memory so CPU can remain busy.

Multitasking

Multiple tasks appear to execute concurrently through CPU scheduling.

Multiprocessing

Uses multiple processors/cores.

Time-sharing

CPU time divided among users/processes for interactive use.

Real-time OS

Designed to meet timing constraints.

